Walnut @ID@: Linux for the PlayStation 3
https://walnut.nanobyte.dev
Full guide: https://github.com/nanobyte-dev/walnut-linux/blob/main/docs/INSTALL.md

Before anything: you need custom firmware with OtherOS++ (Petitboot) and webMAN MOD, a backup of
your flash, and webMAN's fan setting on SYSCON (automatic) or a fixed speed of 60% or more before you
boot OtherOS. The guide explains each.


WHAT'S IN HERE

  walnut-install, lib/stamp.py      the installer (Python 3, nothing else to install)
  dev_hdd0/                         what goes on the PS3's internal disk (GameOS's dev_hdd0)
    linux/walnut.img.xz               the 16 GiB disk image Walnut runs from, compressed
    linux/walnut-good/                a spare copy of the boot files (a recovery copy)
  dev_hdd1/                         what goes on the PS3's small cache partition (dev_hdd1)
    kboot.conf                        Petitboot's menu entry "walnut"
    linux/boot/                       the kernel (vmlinux-@RELEASE@), its arguments, the initramfs
    linux/install/rootfs.tar.xz       the system
    linux/install/install.conf        the install manifest: it goes on LAST
  MANIFEST                          every package in the system, with its version
  SHA256SUMS                        checksums of all of the above


INSTALL ON THE INTERNAL DISK WITH THE INSTALLER (Linux, macOS or Windows with Python 3)

  The PS3 in GameOS, webMAN's FTP server on, the PC on a wired connection:

    python3 walnut-install internal --host <the PS3's IP>      (smaller disk: --size 12G)

  It does every step below, with checks, and makes the image on the fly (no 16 GiB on your PC).


INSTALL ON THE INTERNAL DISK BY HAND (any FTP client, e.g. FileZilla; binary mode)

  1. Check the files: sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS (on Windows: certutil -hashfile <file> SHA256, and
     compare with SHA256SUMS).
  2. Unpack dev_hdd0/linux/walnut.img.xz (xz -dk, or 7-Zip on Windows). The result, walnut.img, must
     be exactly 17179869184 bytes. The PS3 needs that much free space on its internal disk, plus a
     margin.
  3. Connect to the PS3's FTP server (webMAN MOD, port 21, any login).
  4. Upload walnut.img to /dev_hdd0/linux/walnut.img (make the linux folder if it isn't there). Over
     a wired connection this takes about 15 minutes.
  5. Upload the folder dev_hdd0/linux/walnut-good to /dev_hdd0/linux/walnut-good.
  6. Upload the folder dev_hdd1/linux/boot to /dev_hdd1/linux/boot, and
     dev_hdd1/linux/install/rootfs.tar.xz to /dev_hdd1/linux/install/rootfs.tar.xz.
  7. Petitboot's menu: if /dev_hdd1/kboot.conf doesn't exist, upload dev_hdd1/kboot.conf there. If
     it does, download it, add the walnut='...' line from ours (replacing an old walnut= line, if any),
     and upload it back.
  8. Last, upload dev_hdd1/linux/install/install.conf to /dev_hdd1/linux/install/install.conf. The
     first boot installs only when it finds this file, so an upload that stopped halfway never
     installs anything.
  9. Boot OtherOS (fan setting first, see above) and pick "walnut" in Petitboot. The first boot
     checks every block of the image, formats it and unpacks the system (several minutes), then asks
     for your user name and password.

  The image works only as uploaded: don't edit, resize or move walnut.img. To use another size, use
  the installer (--size).


INSTALL ON A USB DISK (a Linux PC; the disk is erased)

    python3 walnut-install usb --disk /dev/sdX

  Then plug it into the PS3, boot OtherOS and pick "walnut-usb" in Petitboot.


Updates come with apt from walnut.nanobyte.dev (@SUITE@).
